  "prompt_text": "The figure is a simple four-step schematic showing the progression of a severe nuclear reactor accident. It begins with normal operation, where the reactor core is intact and cooling appears to be functioning, with cooler blue regions in lower parts of the system and a modest temperature gradient in the core.\n\nThe sequence then shows what happens after loss of both AC and DC power. Without power, the core starts to overheat, indicated by the stronger red color concentrated in the fuel region. As heating continues, the fuel begins to melt. The molten material relocates downward into the lower vessel area, shown in purple, while small dots around it suggest degradation products or released radioactive particles inside the containment.\n\nIn the final stage, the accident escalates to a hydrogen explosion. The containment boundary is shown damaged or open near the top, and many particles are depicted escaping outward, representing release of radioactive materials to the environment. Across the whole figure, the key message is the accident progression from stable reactor conditions to overheating, core melt, structural failure, and eventual release driven by hydrogen generation and explosion.",
